+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+- The original page contained an inline stylesheet and style attribute. Both now live in `/styles.css`; no inline allowances, script nonces, or hashes are necessary.
+- No client-side scripts, DOM injection sinks, cookies, storage, messaging, API calls, embeds, popup integrations, or third-party executable dependencies were found. CSP blocks all scripts, event handlers, eval, plugins, frames and outbound fetches through `default-src 'none'` and explicit directives. Trusted Types is required with no allowed policies in supporting browsers; CSP still protects browsers without Trusted Types support. Future JavaScript must receive a deliberate policy review.
+- The only third-party resource is the existing HTTPS Unsplash background image. Only that image origin is allowed. No third-party script or stylesheet requires SRI. Referrer policy limits cross-origin disclosure to the origin.
+- Responses previously had no browser security headers. The handler now applies CSP, frame protection, nosniff, referrer and permissions restrictions, COOP and CORP to success and error responses. COOP uses `same-origin-allow-popups`; COEP is intentionally omitted because shared-memory isolation is unnecessary and could break the image.
+- Logout previously responded to GET. It now uses a POST form, rejects GET/HEAD, requires the configured Origin, rejects non-same-origin Fetch Metadata, and emits `Clear-Site-Data` only on success. This is still a demonstration endpoint: there are no server sessions to revoke. Real authentication must revoke sessions server-side and use Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite cookies.
+- Fetch Metadata blocks cross-origin resource requests and unsafe methods while allowing normal incoming top-level GET/HEAD navigation. Missing metadata is tolerated for older clients; logout independently requires Origin. No cross-site APIs or webhook exemptions were needed. All responses include the corresponding `Vary` fields and `no-store`.
+
+## Deployment configuration
+
+Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact public origin, such as `https://coffee.example`, without a trailing slash or path. This is required for deployed logout Origin checks and absolute reporting URLs. The local default is `http://localhost:11222` (or the configured PORT). Host and forwarded headers cannot override this setting.
+
+Terminate TLS at the deployment edge, redirect public HTTP traffic to HTTPS there, and restrict direct access to the HTTP backend. The Node server remains an HTTP listener. With an HTTPS public origin, responses advertise an initial `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300`; increase the duration after confirming TLS and redirects. Subdomains and preload are not opted in automatically. Neither HSTS nor modern Reporting API delivery is advertised for the local HTTP default.
+
+`CSP_MODE=enforce` is the default for this audited, script-free page. `CSP_MODE=report-only` keeps frame/base/plugin/form protections enforced and observes the full candidate policy through `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`. Invalid values stop startup rather than silently disabling protection. For deployment changes or new integrations, start with report-only, collect representative real traffic for days to weeks, triage repeat legitimate violations, update the source and policy together, then switch to enforce. Do not add unsafe-inline, unsafe-eval, permissive Trusted Types policies, or broad script allowlists to silence reports.
+
+HTTPS responses configure `Reporting-Endpoints: security="<PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/security-reports"` and `report-to security`. `report-uri /security-reports` provides legacy CSP reporting fallback. Reports are diagnostic, unauthenticated, untrusted, and potentially forged; absence of reports is not proof of compatibility.
+
+The endpoint accepts CSP and Reporting API JSON only, caps each body at 16 KiB and each batch at 20 records, and accepts at most 100 report requests per minute per process. It logs only allowlisted directive names and `report`/`enforce` dispositions. It never logs raw bodies, URLs, samples, referrers, cookies or IP addresses. Configure proxy/body/time/rate limits and log retention at the edge as well; do not enable request-body logging for this endpoint. Multiple processes have independent limits. Filter extension noise before promoting a new policy. Modern reporting requires HTTPS and browser support; delivery can be delayed.
+
+If a CDN serves documents or styles without passing through the Node handler, mirror these headers and MIME types at that layer. Review policy and Fetch Metadata exceptions before adding payments, OAuth, embeds, APIs or browser features.
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm test` exercises the isolated `app.js` request handler using in-memory request/response objects. It never imports `server.js`, opens a listening socket, or launches a browser. Tests cover markup compatibility, headers, CSS MIME type, HEAD responses, staging configuration, origin validation, logout, Fetch Metadata, reporting formats, redaction, payload limits and rate limits. `npm run build` only checks server and handler syntax. No development/preview servers or manual browser verification were used. TLS-edge behavior and actual browser report delivery remain deployment checks.
+
+References: [MDN CSP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP), [Reporting API deployment](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/capabilities/web-apis/reporting-api), [Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data).
